    <!-- ============================================================ 11 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">11</span> Summary &mdash; what to remember</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><strong>RDNA wave width is 32</strong>, not 64. <code>__shfl_*</code>, occupancy math, WMMA <code>_w32</code> builtins all key off this.</li>
        <li><strong>WMMA is the RDNA3+ matrix unit.</strong> Uses <code>v_wmma_*</code> intrinsics, 16&times;16 tiles on RDNA3 (<code>gfx1100/1101/1102/1150/1151</code>), 32-wide tiles on RDNA4 (<code>gfx1200/1201</code> via <code>WMMA32</code>). RDNA1/2 (<code>gfx10xx</code>) have no matrix unit &mdash; the best you get is <code>v_dot2_f32_f16</code> or <code>v_dot4_i32_i8</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>A historical research snapshot reported 2.94&times; higher prefill</strong> for gfx1151 versus gfx1030 on its 9B MQ4 fixture. It is architectural context, not a current benchmark baseline.</li>
        <li><strong>Historical heterogeneous-cluster snapshots showed large gains.</strong> They motivate tier specialization, but are not current end-to-end baselines.</li>
        <li><strong>Dispatch is feature-gated</strong>, not arch-string-matched. New silicon costs one predicate update, not 40 call sites.</li>
        <li><strong>WMMA fused-GEMMs cost ~80&ndash;120 VGPRs/wave.</strong> Fine on RDNA3 (occupancy stays at 10&ndash;12 waves/SIMD); RDNA4's 1.5&times; VGPR file keeps occupancy higher at the same pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Infinity Cache is a uniquely RDNA tier</strong>, sitting between L2 and DRAM at ~256 MB. It widens the effective LLM working set enough that a 24 GB consumer card competes with HBM-class parts at short context.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

